---
import { getImage, Image } from 'astro:assets';
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import PhotoHero from '../../components/PhotoHero.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import Split from '../../components/Split.astro';
import Gallery from '../../components/Gallery.astro';
import QuoteSection from '../../components/QuoteSection.astro';
import { img } from '../../lib/images';

const heroImage = img('farm/hero.jpg');
const ogImage = await getImage({ src: heroImage, width: 1200, format: 'webp' });

const permacultureImage = img('retreats/farm_animals.jpg');

const galleryItems = [
  { image: img('farm/property_overview.jpg'), alt: 'Aerial view of the farm property in the cloud forest', size: 'lg' as const },
  { image: img('farm/gallery_1.jpg'), alt: 'Farm landscape at Wild on the Farm' },
  { image: img('farm/gallery_2.jpg'), alt: 'Farm landscape at Wild on the Farm', size: 'tall' as const },
  { image: img('farm/overview_1.jpg'), alt: 'Overview of the cloud forest farm', size: 'wide' as const },
  { image: img('farm/gallery_4.jpg'), alt: 'Farm landscape at Wild on the Farm' },
  { image: img('farm/pergola.jpg'), alt: 'Pergola on the farm property' },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="The Farm — Wild on the Farm | Boquete Cloud Forest Lodge"
  description="Eighteen years of organic, permaculture farming at 5,900 feet in Panama's cloud forest, on the edge of Amistad National Park."
  path="/es/finca/"
  image={ogImage.src}
  pullIntro="Nestled at 5,900 feet in the cloud forest of Amistad National Park, our farm is a haven of peace, silence, and cool mountain air. Its stillness is almost monastic — secluded in wild nature, it invites regeneration, a reconnection with life's most essential elements."
  locale="es"
>
  <Fragment slot="hero">
    <PhotoHero image={heroImage} alt="The farm at Wild on the Farm, Boquete" eyebrow="The Farm">
      Eighteen years in the ground, before the first guest ever arrived.
    </PhotoHero>
  </Fragment>

  <Section label="In Photographs" heading="A landscape that was here long before we were.">
    <Gallery items={galleryItems} groupId="the-farm" />
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <Split>
      <div>
        <div class="section-label">Since 2008</div>
        <h2 class="lede">A working farm, long before it was a place to stay.</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Wild on the Farm was never built to look like a farm. It has been one since 2008 — a
          certified organic farm and macadamia plantation, cultivated in patient harmony with the
          cloud forest that surrounds it.
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2025, three handcrafted cabins opened their doors for the first time. Everything
          else — the soil, the trees, the rhythm of the seasons — was already here, eighteen years
          in the making.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <Split>
      <div>
        <div class="section-label">A Life Built on Permaculture</div>
        <h2 class="lede">Working with the mountain, not against it.</h2>
        <p>
          The farm runs entirely off the grid. Solar panels provide our electricity, and our
          water is carried from a spring that rises straight out of the rock, a mile and a half
          inside Amistad National Park.
        </p>
        <p>
          Over the years, we've shaped the hillside into brick-lined terraces where vegetables,
          aromatic herbs, and medicinal plants grow in rotation. A small flock of sheep grazes
          beneath the macadamia and fruit trees by day — clearing undergrowth and fertilizing the
          soil as they go — before returning each evening to the safety of their fold, away from
          predators. Their manure, along with that of our hens, is composted and returned to the
          land.
        </p>
        <p>
          We make our own liquid plant feeds, too: nettle, comfrey, and tithonia, fermented into
          natural fertilizers that keep the terraces fed without anything brought in from outside.
          The same nettle finds its way into our kitchen — in soups, and in the teas served at the
          table.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-photo">
        <Image
          src={permacultureImage}
          alt="Sheep and hens grazing together beneath the macadamia trees"
          widths={[400, 700, 1000]}
          sizes="(min-width: 860px) 50vw, 100vw"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <Split>
      <div>
        <div class="section-label">More Than a Retreat</div>
        <h2 class="lede">A living farm first, a sanctuary for many kinds of travelers.</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          The stillness here isn't reserved for one kind of guest. It's the same silence that
          sharpens a birder's ear at dawn, that makes a hiking trail feel longer and better, and
          that turns a simple meal from the garden into something worth slowing down for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <QuoteSection cite="— Pascal, Founder">
    "After years spent running a hotel in Quito, Pascal chose to return to something slower and
    rooted — turning this working farm into a sanctuary for travelers drawn to the same quiet."
  </QuoteSection>

  <Fragment slot="footer">
    <div class="section-label">Continue Exploring</div>
    <h2>Where the farm leads next.</h2>
    <p>See where you'll sleep, what's grown for your table, and the trails that start at our door.</p>
    <div class="contact-list">
      <a href="/es/lodge/">The Cabins →</a>
      <a href="/es/comida/">The Food →</a>
      <a href="/es/avistamiento-de-aves/">Avistamiento de Aves →</a>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</PageLayout>
